  <h1 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
    5G and IoT: Supply Chain Analysis, Security Implications, and Their Impact on US-China Trade
  </h1>
  <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">Hudson Institute · August 2019</p>

  <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
    The following is an excerpt from a longer research synthesis mapping the security, trade, and
    supply-chain dynamics of the US-China race for 5G and IoT dominance, pulling together patent
    filings, breaking news, and legislation to inform strategy. The excerpt below shows how national
    policy toward Huawei diverged across allied governments and why.
  </p>

  <blockquote class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-4 border-l-4 border-neutral-300 pl-5 text-neutral-700 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
    <p>
      Of the Five Eyes alliance, New Zealand, Australia, and the United States are the only members
      which have outright banned Huawei from their 5G infrastructure, although recent events
      concerning the United States have put the ban into jeopardy. Australia led the charge with
      banning Huawei in 2018 following an investigation into the security of their devices. Australia
      then shared the information with the United States and other countries, urging them to tighten
      their 5G supply chain. The difference in attitude between Australia and the UK can be traced to
      their historical relationship with the Chinese company. Since 2012, Australia has been wary of
      the company and prevented them from participating in the National Broadband Network project.
      This has been attributed to China's growing influence in Australia, as it has had issues with
      China's political influence in the past, including donations to particular election campaigns.
      In addition to the 5G ban, Australia also recently introduced legislation to combat foreign
      influence in politics.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the other hand, the UK has been using Huawei equipment in its major networks, Vodafone and
      BT Group, for years, and have even cooperated with Huawei to create a cybersecurity evaluation
      center in order to ease security concerns.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

